WBFit 1.0 - NativeCall PRIVATE TEST BUILD
=========================================

THIS IS A PRIVATE TEST BUILD. Do not pass it on, upload it or put it in any
archive: it contains code from the NativeCall SDK (ncsupport.c, and the
module loader's conventions), which has no licence yet (all rights reserved
by its author). The public build is the WBFit in Utilities.lha (no
NativeCall).

What it is
----------

The same WBFit (same arguments, same files, same prefs), built for the 68040
with a NativeCall module (wbfit.narm, embedded) that runs its pixel and byte
work as AArch64 code on the Raspberry Pi under Emu68:

  hpass    resampling across (bicubic, widened when shrinking) (C)
  vpass    resampling down, 16 bytes at a time (NEON)
  fill     the FIT/CENTER background (C)
  lut      old picture.datatype's palette/HAM pixels to RGB (C)
  hist     the histogram for the median cut (C)
  dither   nearest colour / Floyd-Steinberg to the palette (C)
  pngfilt  the PNG row filters (best of five per row) (C)
  deflate  the PNG's zlib stream: hash chains, dynamic/fixed/stored
           blocks, resumable (C)
  crc      the PNG chunk CRCs (C)
  ilbm     chunky to bitplanes and ByteRun1 (C)

Reading the picture (datatypes), the median cut itself, the file and the
prefs stay on the 68k. Each native call stops after about 0.3 ms (the ARM
counter) and is called again from where it stopped, so the 68k never waits
more than about that; one ARM core.

Every kernel gives the same bytes as the 68k code (tested under
qemu-aarch64_be with the module itself, NEON included: random rows and
weights for the passes, deflate on several kinds of data, and whole jobs of
every mode and format compared stage by stage). All the arithmetic is
integer, so native and 68k results are identical, not just close.

It falls back to the 68k code when there is no nativecall.library, no
NativeCall armstub, no 68040/68060, or a buffer native code can't see. If a
native call ever fails, that stage is redone from its start with the 68k
code and native calls stay off for the rest of the run (the output says
so).

Trying it
---------

  Copy WBFit C:WBFit-nc           (or anywhere; it is one file)

  WBFit-nc Pics:photo.jpg BENCH
  WBFit-nc Pics:photo.webp MODE=FIT BENCH
  WBFit-nc Pics:photo.jpg FORMAT=ILBM DITHER BENCH

BENCH fits the picture with the native kernels (and sets the backdrop as
usual), then does the same work again with the 68k code (without writing),
and prints a table: decode (through datatypes, not ours: the same both
ways), scale, dither (palette ILBM only), encode (PNG filter + deflate +
CRC, or ILBM), our stages together, and the total; for each the native and
68k time, the gain, and whether the results are the same (a CRC of each
stage's output). Then each kernel's native calls from the hypervisor's own
clock: calls, total, average and longest call (every call should stay
under about 1000 us; a "!" marks one that did not).

  WBFit-nc Pics:photo.jpg TIMES   only the native run, a line per stage

What to expect
--------------

On a 1920x1080 RTG Workbench from a large JPEG, the 68k code under Emu68
spends most of its time in deflate and the two scaling passes. As native
code those should be several times faster (deflate and the scaler perhaps
3-8x, the NEON vertical pass more); the decode by the datatype is not ours
and does not change. BENCH on your own machine is the real measure:
please send its table.
